Quickstart
Draw a specific lived moment with confident ink, soft forms, believable contact and room to colour. Handmade appearance comes from the decisions in the drawing: the pose, the silhouette, the placement of objects and the way one curve continues into another.
Choose the task before doing work
Request	Read and apply	Produce
One new illustration	Operating contract, reference roles, human-hand mechanics, line work, relevant mascot rules and prompt architecture	An actual image, then a concise review note
A change to an existing image	The edit target, invariants, relevant repair instruction and affected quality checks	A targeted image edit that preserves accepted features
A new book batch	The above, plus continuity, complexity balance and production workflow	A calibrated pilot followed by the requested pages
A written drawing guide or prompt	The relevant rules and requested output scope	The requested writing; do not generate extra artwork automatically
For an illustration, follow this loop:
Identify which images control character identity, line character and environment. Reuse existing approved references.
State the visible action and one particular observation. Decide the contact between character, prop and support surface.
Choose a large composition: the action silhouette, one framing shape, two setting anchors and a quiet area. Remove unrelated inventory.
Decide the gesture before adding facial detail. The body, gaze and prop must support the same action.
Use the accepted ink profile. For a new book, calibrate it once at final size. Do not hard-code a pen nib description into every image as a physical stroke measurement.
Compile a compact four-part prompt. Include the relevant references and only the restrictions that matter for this page.
Generate the image and inspect the actual result. Repair the largest visible defect with a focused edit, then inspect again.
Record the checks actually performed. Keep untested work labelled as a draft and deliver the best completed stage within the request.
Do not paste this whole document into an image prompt. The directing agent reads the guide and compiles the page brief. Composition, image-tool execution and print preparation are different jobs; a long list of millimetres will not make an image generator into a print-layout engine.
Agent operating contract
Priority when instructions conflict
Use this order:
The user's current scene request and explicit corrections.
Approved character images and approved continuity decisions.
Hard requirements in this document.
The page's story brief.
Optional composition, colour and scene suggestions.
General inspiration from external references.
An approved character image controls identity; it does not authorize reproducing another publisher's page composition. A story brief controls the event; it does not require keeping an accidental drawing defect.
This document's line and density settings supersede earlier provisional art settings in the Miso and Friends book plan. Its story, page order and cast are retained unless the user changes them.
Hard requirements
Preserve the named mascots' identities and approved proportions.
Use clear, rounded, heavy contours. No scratchy construction lines, accidental double tracing or decorative line noise.
Every intended colourable region must have a closed visible boundary, including boundaries completed by a legitimate overlap.
In COLOURING_MASTER, use black ink and a white background, with no intentional gray fills, colour, shading or photographic texture.
Make the action readable without depending on a caption, a particular colour palette or a tiny object.
Keep foreground faces and the main interaction clear of background lines.
Give props stable support and plausible connections. Hands, handles, wheels, ropes and furniture must connect correctly.
Type captions and signs in a separate layout step. Do not accept model-generated pseudo-writing.
Create an original scene. Use external references to understand principles, not as art to trace or reproduce.
Do not label generated artwork as drawn by a human artist. A handmade appearance is an aesthetic target, not a statement about authorship.
Produce the requested artifact. When asked for a drawing, do not stop at a written prompt or replace the illustration with a coded assembly of simple shapes.
Treat identity and continuity as fixed; treat pose, crop and deliberate object asymmetry as expressive choices. Never randomize facial proportions to create a handmade effect.
Keep test claims precise. A requested property is not a verified property, and a self-assigned score does not replace looking at the image.
Select exactly one output mode
Mode	Required appearance	What to exclude
COLOURING_MASTER	Black line art; white paper; large unfilled regions; no caption baked into the illustration	Colours, gray shading, marker streaks, paper grain, mockup backgrounds
COLOURED_PREVIEW	A separate coloured version using the accepted master's exact contours and simple flat fills	Redesigning anatomy, adding new objects or inventing extra outlines to make the preview impressive
COVER_ART	Coloured, original hero scene; clear focal cast; space for separately added title	Photographing an imaginary finished product, borrowed logos, a crowded catalogue of unrelated props
ACTIVITY_MASTER	Clean outline scaffolding with intentionally empty drawing space	Filling the spaces the child is meant to complete
If no mode is supplied, use COLOURING_MASTER. Cover colours must never enter the colouring master.
Image tools and reference roles
Use a real image workflow
For a new raster illustration, use the available dedicated image-generation tool. For a requested modification, use its image-edit workflow when available. Inspect local input images before using them. Follow the actual tool's documented reference-image mechanism and supported parameters.
Do not invent a model, quality flag, mask control, seed option, destination argument or reference-strength slider. If the built-in image tool is available, use it rather than writing an SDK runner. Follow the host's authorization requirements before switching to a paid or separately configured API route.
Do not substitute SVG clip art, circles and rectangles drawn by code, HTML, CSS or a Python painting script for the requested handmade-looking illustration. Code is useful for layout, metadata and quality analysis. An illustrator can use a native vector drawing application to make expressive paths, but a programmatic shape collage is a different result. If the user explicitly requests editable vector artwork, respect that format and judge its drawing quality by the same rules.
Label references by function
Input role	What it controls	What it must not accidentally control
Character identity sheet	Silhouette, facial language, anatomy, accessories and comparative scale	Every future pose, expression or composition
Accepted line-art pilot	Ink character, simplification and relative detail	A duplicate scene arrangement on every page
Environment reference	Door, window, furniture and recurring spatial relationships	A single frozen camera angle
External inspiration	Broad observations about shape, warmth or staging	Our mascot identity, another publisher's exact layout, logos or lettering
Edit target	The accepted image being changed	Permission to redesign unaffected parts
When the tool uses numbered images, say what each numbered image controls. Use the smallest set that supplies the necessary information. A photograph of a book page includes paper shadows, perspective and perhaps watermarks: those are reference context, not desired drawing features. Never present a competitor's cleaned or traced page as an original production asset.
For a continuing book, an accepted original pilot becomes the main line-style reference. Repeatedly mixing unrelated reference pages can pull the drawing into different styles.
Before a targeted edit
Name the edit target, the region or object to change and the invariants. Repeat the essential invariants in each follow-up: cast identity, expression unless being edited, major geometry, important prop counts, room continuity and accepted ink character. Keep a known-good version so a failed edit can be rolled back.
