What the additional research contributes
Coco Wyo separates simpler and more detailed collections. Its own descriptions support bold outlines and larger colouring spaces; its brand name does not describe one single complexity level. [S1–S3]
A current Coco Wyo gallery image shows a finished coloured example with additional surface effects. Treat such effects as a colouring treatment, not evidence that they belong in the blank master. [S4]
Bobbie Goods demonstrates a recurring-character sequence built around daily activities. Its drawing guide also treats characters, objects and scenes as learnable components. [S5–S6]
Usborne's first-colouring range supports a simpler large-area approach for younger children. We should simplify further for a preschool edition rather than merely reusing a 6–9 page. [S7]
Crayola's picnic activity leaves content for the child to invent. Selected pages in our book should allow that kind of participation. [S8]
Highlights shows that colouring and optional visual discovery can coexist. Our small search motif remains secondary to colouring. [S9]
Procreate documents both stroke smoothing and controls that retain expression. Clean digital finishing is compatible with human-looking marks. [S10]
The uploaded cocowyo-design-dna(1).md is a supporting reference. Its website font sizes, UI colours and CSS claims are not drawing measurements and were not independently re-measured for this document. Do not import its website layout, buttons or Nunito typography into the illustration system. Where it suggests dense miniature rooms or zero pressure variation, use the current child-focused density and restrained variation rules below.
Creative philosophy
A place the child can imagine entering
Show a moment that feels comfortable and specific: a seat made ready, a snack saved for a friend, a paper decoration that hangs slightly crooked. Let the picture suggest what happened just before it and what might happen next.
The illustration should feel drawn by someone who noticed ordinary things. A large berry on Sprout's tray is more memorable than five unrelated floating hearts. A cushion pressed under a character communicates softness without texture rendering.
What makes a page feel premium
Selection: each object earns its place through the action, the setting or a character detail.
Hierarchy: the first thing the reader sees is the intended action.
Finish: faces, contours, joins and repeated props are consistent.
Specificity: the page contains a small observation that could not be swapped into any generic cute scene.
Restraint: the illustrator stops before the empty areas become cluttered.
Richness comes from meaningful relationships between shapes. An image with only six objects can feel complete when those objects describe a believable moment.
Handmade appearance
Use long, confident contours, modest differences between paired forms and a few naturally uneven arrangements. Keep the difference between a relaxed human decision and a drawing error clear.
Keep	Repair
A head that is gently wider on one side	A face that changes identity between pages
One scarf tassel sitting slightly higher	Tassels fused into the arm
Two cups with slightly different rounded handles	A handle that fails to connect to the cup
A blanket with a softly curled corner	Impossible folds that become extra limbs
A smooth hand-swept oval	A trembling, vibrating or sawtoothed outline
A deliberate loose arrangement	Accidental tangencies and floating furniture
Avoid adding random jitter, speckle, fake scanning noise or distressed ink to make a clean drawing seem handmade. Raster and vector tools are both acceptable. The issue is the visible result, not whether the software uses Bézier curves.
How the drawing acquires a human hand
Begin with the action through the body
Sketch the relationship mentally or in an internal working draft before finishing the contour. A character should lean toward the dough it is kneading, settle into the cushion it sits on or turn toward the friend it is listening to. A face added to an upright bean does not establish a convincing action.
For each pose identify:
The surface bearing the character's weight.
The direction of the head and body.
The point where a paw, star point or snail tray meets the important prop.
The gaze or head orientation toward that action.
These are art-direction decisions. They do not require making a separate sketch image for every page or delivering construction lines in the master.
Use a small vocabulary of deliberate curves
Drawing decision	How it should look	Failure to avoid
Long outside contour	One confident broad sweep around a cheek, loaf, cushion or shell	Many short bumps pasted together to imitate drawing
Gentle change of curvature	A soft cheek becomes a chin without a mechanical corner	A lumpy or melted silhouette with no clear form
Paired shapes	Related ears, paws or handles with modest natural differences	Mirror-copy symmetry, or mismatched anatomy
Resting surface	Slight compression or flattening where weight meets a cushion or floor	A rigid mascot hovering above its seat
Fold	One or two curves explaining the cloth's direction	Decorative wrinkle webs unrelated to the pose
Repeated objects	Similar cups or paper moons with purposeful variation	A cloned stamp repeated at identical intervals
Keep the silhouette understandable when internal decoration is removed. Do not try to rescue a weak silhouette with blush, sparkles or tiny texture.
Put asymmetry where it has a reason
Choose one or two expressive differences in an ordinary scene: one ear responds to the head turn, a scarf end hangs lower, a cushion compresses under weight or one paper decoration tilts on its string. Let the rest of the picture remain quiet.
Do not make every circle crooked, every line wavy and every shelf slanted. Built objects still have structure. Human-looking art can contain a straight tabletop or a round cup opening; it should not look mechanically cloned throughout.
Separate softness from inflation
A soft form still has a useful shape. Give a mitten paw a clear gripping side; let the book remain a book; preserve the kitten's triangular ears and Pip's five points. Rounding every feature into a balloon erases identity and makes props difficult to read.
Show evidence of life
Choose a detail that follows from the action: a folded cloth beside a washed cup, a compressed cushion, a saved snack or a picture propped where a friend can see it. Include enough disorder to suggest use, while keeping the colouring spaces clear. Random clutter and evenly scattered decorations do not serve this purpose.
