Line work
Calibrate the marker feel at final size
Keep the requested 1.5–2 mm felt-tip pen feel as a medium description. Nib size, the width of a laid-down mark, photographed pixels and final printed width are different quantities. The previous default of a literal 1.5 mm contour on every page is replaced by a small calibration range: the stroke must remain bold without closing faces, handles or useful colouring regions.
For a new 6.75-inch-square art panel, use 1.2 mm as a provisional primary contour, then compare approximately 1.0, 1.2 and 1.5 mm treatments at final size if the source or drawing tool allows direct stroke control. Choose the treatment closest to the desired boldness that preserves character detail and broad fill areas. An explicit user request for a physical width overrides these starting settings.
Physical width at final size	Approximate points	Approximate pixels at 300 DPI	Initial use
1.0 mm	2.83 pt	11.81 px	Lower calibration sample; some secondary structure
1.2 mm	3.40 pt	14.17 px	Provisional main contour before a pilot is accepted
1.5 mm	4.25 pt	17.72 px	Heavier calibration sample or an approved bold treatment
2.0 mm	5.67 pt	23.62 px	A deliberately heavy treatment for very simple large forms
Conversion: pixels = millimetres × DPI / 25.4. These are house starting values, not measured publisher specifications. A text instruction alone cannot enforce a precise width on a generated bitmap. If direct stroke control is unavailable, compare the visible result with the accepted pilot at the same placed size and record it as a visual match rather than a measured width.
Once a pilot is accepted, reuse its line character across the book. Do not run a new width experiment for every scene or mechanically thicken an otherwise good page just to satisfy a number.
Use a quiet hierarchy
Primary character and foreground contours share one dominant weight. Internal structure can use the same weight or be slightly lighter where needed. Secondary background lines may start around 80% of the primary weight; they still need to print clearly. Small facial dots follow the approved sheet rather than being forced to equal the stroke diameter.
Do not emphasize every outer edge with a second black border. Avoid thick stickers around characters unless that is a separately requested asset. Depth comes from overlap and placement, with restrained line hierarchy instead of shadows.
Contour behaviour
At normal viewing distance, the page should read as nearly monoline.
Allow subtle, smooth differences in a curve's pressure or shape where they support the drawing. Do not add periodic width changes or random noise.
Use rounded joins and ends, without bulbous black dots at every endpoint.
Let one broad contour describe a cheek or cushion. Keep transitions smooth instead of joining many small lumps.
Keep a tool or furniture edge purposeful. Straight structure is compatible with a handmade drawing.
Remove ghost outlines, accidental double tracing, broken edges and hairline spikes.
Use broad fur lobes only where the approved mascot requires them.
Keep repeated line junctions clean; no heavy black knots at paws, handles or sleeves.
Interior marks
Use the fewest marks that explain form, material or action. A cushion may need one seam, a loaf a few broad score marks and a closed book one page-block line. Remove marks that merely subdivide otherwise pleasant areas.
If an enclosed detail pinches shut at the chosen weight, enlarge or simplify it. A targeted lighter internal line can help when it preserves the book's established ink character; making the entire page delicate is not the default repair.
Stroke calibration review
Inspect a small representative sample containing a face, paw-and-cup contact, blanket fold, shell spiral and window edge. At actual size, all must remain recognizable and have usable white separation. Choose and record one ink profile. No sample images or printed tests are claimed merely by following this written procedure.
Colourable regions and clean intersections
Closure applies to regions
An intended fill region must be sealed by its visible boundaries. A shape behind another object can be closed by that foreground object's boundary; its hidden outline must not continue through the foreground object.
A smile, eyebrow, page crease, motion mark or short fold can be an intentional open stroke. Do not close those marks into little tubes merely to satisfy a literal interpretation of “all lines closed.”
Occlusion and contact
Decide which object is in front.
Draw the front object's outline as the visible boundary.
Stop the rear object's outline cleanly at that boundary.
Remove the hidden line through the foreground object.
Check the resulting fill region and the physical connection.
For a paw holding a cup, the paw must touch the cup or its handle in a plausible place. Keep enough outline to distinguish paw from ceramic. Do not make a single black knot where fingers, rim, handle and sleeve meet.
At an attachment, fuse the geometry cleanly where required while retaining the object's identity. “Seamless” does not mean welding every touching prop into one blob.
The tangency check
Avoid an ear just touching a window bar, a cup rim kissing a cheek, a hanging string meeting an eye or a plant leaf that looks like an extra limb. Move one form, increase the gap or introduce an unmistakable overlap.
For unrelated adjacent forms, aim for a visible white separation at least twice the primary stroke width; enlarge it near faces. This is an initial layout target. Deliberate touching and overlapping forms follow the contact rules instead.
Digital fill test
When digital colouring compatibility is requested:
Make a temporary high-resolution test copy. Do not destructively threshold the production master.
Fill the outside background, then independently test the main character body, clothing, important props and enclosed environment regions.
Check for spill into a neighbouring region, into the background or through an intersection.
Inspect narrow diagonal boundaries; if tooling permits, check both four-neighbour and eight-neighbour connectivity on the temporary mask.
Repair the actual junction and repeat. Do not rely on an application's aggressive gap-closing option to disguise a defect.
Small antialiasing pixels at a smooth black edge are acceptable. Intentional gray areas, gray halos and soft blurred contours are not. Fill tolerances differ by app, so record the app or mask test used; do not promise universal bucket-tool compatibility without checking it.
Character construction
Preserve the character before applying a formula
Use the approved drawing's head shape, eye relationship, ears, body and accessories. Do not rebuild the existing mascots from a generic kawaii ratio. For a genuinely new rounded animal, a large head and short rounded limbs may be useful, but a new character requires its own coherent design.
Avoid distinct knuckles, tendons, wrists and realistic elbows. A paw can be a rounded mitten with a clear contact edge. Preserve enough shape to distinguish a gripping paw from a foot or a cheek.
Pip and Sprout have separate construction rules. Pip is a five-point star, not an animal head on a body. Sprout has one attached shell, a soft body and two eye stalks, with no hands.
Give the pose a purpose
Action	Body and contact decision
Reading	Head and gaze turn toward the book; paws support its sides or lower edge
Kneading	Body leans slightly toward the work surface; rounded paws clearly press the dough
Passing food	Giver, food and receiver form a readable path; separate overlapping paws
Sitting	Base rests on the cushion or floor; fabric compresses a little beneath weight
Listening	Head and body orient toward the friend; do not force every face toward the viewer
Pip peeking	Use the star's existing points to establish the pose; keep enough silhouette visible to identify five-point construction
Sprout carrying	Tray rests on the shell or an established support; the snail does not gain limbs
Keep a clear difference between expressive lean and impossible balance. Do not make a character's head, paws and accessory all react in unrelated directions.
Facial minimalism with expression
Keep the approved small dot eyes or closed arcs and existing nose-and-mouth construction. Use the model sheet's relative face placement instead of a repeated coordinate formula. A head turn changes how the features project; it should not accidentally change the character's face.
Use head tilt, ear angle, gaze and posture before enlarging a mouth or adding eyebrows. A small asymmetry in a smile can be expressive, but facial identity must remain stable. Avoid glossy eyes, large pupil highlights and elaborate cheek contouring unless already part of the design.
Emotional range
Use interest, concentration, mild disappointment, relief, pride or sleepiness when the story needs it. Miso can look disappointed by the rain. Plum can concentrate while placing a cup. Pip can look pleased with an uneven decoration. A content scene does not require every face to wear the same pasted-on smile.
Clothing and accessories
Miso's scarf is an identity feature. Keep its basic design stable, with its drape responding to pose. Temporary clothing should support weather or action and remain simple. Broad sweater bands, a cuff or a hem are usually enough; avoid knitting maps, tiny buttons and needless costume changes.
Do not use accessories to conceal a broken pose or a changing character silhouette.
