Turn sheet requirements
Before a long batch, establish front, three-quarter and side views, one seated or resting pose, and at least three needed expressions. Show the characters at a common scale on one sheet. The directing agent should reuse that sheet throughout the project.
Preserve head silhouette, eye placement, ears, scarf shape, star points and shell structure. A fixed seed can help repeat a composition, but it does not guarantee identity. An approved image reference is more useful than repeating the name alone.
Interaction rules
Let the friends look toward the object or person involved in the action.
Keep Sprout's places accessible at floor or low-table level.
When a character passes an item, show a clear giver, item and receiver.
Limit a difficult contact pose to one focal interaction; simplify the rest of the scene.
In a group, preserve individual silhouettes. Do not hide one mascot merely to fit a crowded composition.
Let personality emerge from choices: Miso draws, Pip improvises, Plum makes space and Sprout notices.
Scene invention
Write the moment before drawing the room
Fill these fields:
Field	Required decision
Visible action	What exactly is the character doing with its body or a prop?
Emotional reason	Why does this moment matter to the character?
Place	Which two or three shapes make the location recognizable?
Particular detail	What affectionate, funny or surprising observation makes this scene memorable?
Shape anchor	Which large silhouette holds the composition together?
Quiet area	Where can the viewer rest or the child add colour freely?
Weak brief: “Cute animals in a cozy kitchen.”
Usable brief: “Miso kneads one big mound of dough while Pip, dusted with two flour patches, peers over the bowl. A rounded worktop and broad cupboard establish the kitchen. Pip's tiny star-shaped dough piece sits beside Miso's much larger bun.”
One useful twist
Select one primary creative device for an ordinary page:
Scale: a berry seems enormous beside Sprout.
Small mishap: a paper moon hangs the wrong way up.
Care: Plum puts the smallest cup where Sprout can reach it.
Adaptation: a rainy porch leads to an indoor picnic.
Evidence of an activity: the drawing, bowl or folded cloth shows what a character has been doing.
Shared attention: two friends stop to watch one falling leaf.
Gentle transformation: an empty corner becomes a reading place using a cushion and blanket.
Keep the event legible before adding a secondary joke. Do not fill every surface with faces, hearts, bows, stars or random mushrooms.
Make the ordinary particular
Use combinations such as a cup saved on a saucer, one uneven paper decoration, two slippers facing different directions or a bookmark made from a leaf. Choose two details that describe the same activity. Remove decorative objects that suggest a different story.
Fantasy can change the premise, such as a star friend joining a picnic. Within that premise, keep support, scale and contact understandable. A basket needs a bottom; an umbrella shaft needs to meet its canopy.
The removal test
For each prop, ask whether removing it would lose the action, setting or particular detail. Keep a few atmosphere props if they create a deliberate rhythm. Delete the rest. A crowded image does not become premium merely because it contains more things.
