Eye flow
The focal action should read first at a small thumbnail. A broad window, table edge or blanket curve may lead toward it. A secondary object should reward a closer look. Keep the background quieter than the foreground interaction.
Do not give every prop the same size or visual importance. Use a few large shapes and some medium ones. Very small details should be rare and purposeful.
Perspective
Use a straight-on view or a shallow three-quarter view. A simple illustration can show depth without being a technical perspective drawing, but one table cannot have contradictory edge directions or legs attached to its top face.
Use overlap, placement and modest scale changes to separate planes. No shading is needed. Avoid fisheye views, dramatic foreshortening and multiple competing vanishing directions.
Framing
A gently irregular rounded frame can make a complete vignette. Keep it well inside the page's safe area and leave visible white between it and unrelated contours. It must not crop ears, paws, star points or the key prop. A scene can also stand on a simple ground line; do not force a box around every page.
Props and material language
Use familiar objects with slightly softened geometry. Keep the functional feature that makes each object identifiable.
Material or object	Draw enough to communicate it	Leave out
Cup or bowl	One clear opening, broad body and connected rounded handle if needed	Several concentric rims or microscopic reflections
Bread	Pillowy outer contour and two or three broad score marks	Crumb dots, pores and photorealistic crust
Berries	One large fruit silhouette with a few broad seed or leaf shapes	Dozens of tiny pips and realistic surface texture
Blanket	Soft outer contour, one or two useful folds and an optional broad border	Dense checks, fringe forests or a pattern in every cell
Cushion	Rounded block, one seam and slight compression under weight	Tufting grids, heavy shadow or repeated stitching
Sweater	Broad silhouette, cuff, hem and perhaps two large stripes	Knitted stitches and fine ribbing
Wood furniture	Round-ended boards, believable joins, one broad grain curve if useful	Repeated grain lines covering every surface
Book	Soft rectangle, one spine or page-block boundary, simple cover	Tiny pseudo-text and many parallel page lines
Glass	Clear vessel contour and one meaningful liquid boundary	Gray transparency wash and disconnected duplicate rims
Metal tool	Familiar outline and a readable connection or handle	Shiny gradients, tiny screws and excess gleam marks
Plant	A few large leaves with one useful vein each	Intricate serrations, dozens of stems and filler foliage
Water	Broad bounded areas, a few large ripple curves, clear object overlap	Fine wave hatching or endless nested contours
Basket or bag	Rounded main volume, one handle and a simple large panel	Full woven lattice, repeated zips and tiny buckles
Umbrella	Clear canopy, a few broad panels and one connected shaft	Hairline ribs, many spokes and confusing shaft intersections
Window	One recognizable opening and minimal structural divisions	A grid across a character's face or overlapping impossible frames
Paper craft	Slightly uneven broad shape and a readable fold or suspension	Elaborate micro-cuts and many dangling thin strings
Faces on objects
Miso, Pip, Plum and Sprout already provide character. An occasional friendly plant pot can be charming, but do not add a face to every snack, utensil and piece of furniture. Make the agent identify any personified prop in the scene brief before drawing it.
Text-like content
Use simple picture blocks to establish a menu, newspaper or notebook. Keep the marks sparse. If readable words are needed, reserve a blank sign for separately typeset or deliberately hand-lettered text after the illustration is accepted. A generated squiggle that resembles an alphabet is a defect.
Density and difficulty
The crayon test
The child should have satisfying areas to fill with the side of a crayon or a broad pencil stroke. At final print size:
Keep most deliberate fill areas at least about 8–12 mm across at their narrowest useful part. This is a house usability target to test, not a clinical rule.
Give important standalone props a recognizable footprint around 18–25 mm or larger. Enlarge the prop if its identity depends on a smaller detail.
Aim to leave at least 70% of a major object's interior visibly open and unpatterned. A few broad dividing lines can still create large fill regions.
Exempt eyes, mouths, necessary tiny attachments and the optional search motif from the large-area rule. Do not multiply those exceptions into filler detail.
Open white space inside objects is different from an unfinished composition. A fully arranged scene can remain mostly white before colouring.
Treat the percentages as a visual review guide unless actual measurement was performed. Do not claim a computed open-area ratio from an unmeasured image.
Three complexity levels
Level	Starting cast and prop budget	Character of the page
EASY	1 main character; 2–4 functional props; about 10–20 obvious medium or large fill regions	A large action or portrait with very little environment
STANDARD	1–2 main characters; 4–6 functional props; about 20–35 fill regions	A clear activity with one simple setting
RICH	2–4 characters; 6–8 carefully chosen props; about 30–45 fill regions	A story milestone with stronger staging, not miniature detail
These are rough planning counts. Enclosure testing may find many tiny incidental regions that the concept budget did not anticipate; remove those rather than rationalizing them as detail.
For a 40-scene collection, an initial mix could be 12 easy, 22 standard and 6 richer pages. An existing narrative can use another balance. Do not add unrelated locations or change story order just to satisfy a quota.
If density is too high: remove texture, consolidate repeated props, enlarge key objects, then simplify the setting. Do not immediately shrink the cast or make the main outline delicate.
Younger readers
For a separate preschool edition, use much simpler, larger single actions and fewer objects. Test with that audience. Do not assume a rich page becomes preschool-friendly because its outline is thick. The current project remains ages 6–9.
Colour and cover direction
Colouring master
Use intentional black marks on white. No pre-coloured blush, gray fur, gray water, tinted page, lighting bloom, drop shadow or stipple. Colour choices belong to the child.
Coloured previews
Keep the approved outline geometry unchanged and put fills beneath it. Start with flat colour. A coloured preview is a separate derivative; it must not be passed off as an additional uncoloured page. Reusing a locked line layer preserves its geometry; asking a generator to recolour may alter it, so inspect that result and do not claim pixel-identical contours without comparison.
Do not automatically add highlights and marker effects just because a published gallery shows a skilled colourist's finished page. If an embellished demonstration is explicitly requested, keep it separate from both the master and the simple cover palette.
