The panel dimensions are a starting layout, not an instruction to crop a composition into a square after it has been drawn. Compose for the final panel. Mirror the inner-margin allowance appropriately on left-hand pages.
Export discipline
Preserve an editable or layered working master when the tool supports it.
Export clean PNG or another lossless working image; use PDF for the assembled print interior.
Flatten export transparency onto white for a normal print master. Keep a separate transparent line layer only when needed for a digital colouring deliverable.
Inspect the effective resolution at final placed size. Changing a DPI metadata tag does not add missing pixels or recover detail.
Avoid JPEG for the line-art master because compression may add halos and damaged edges.
Keep grayscale edge antialiasing controlled. Avoid aggressive thresholding that turns smooth curves jagged.
Do not include printer marks, watermarks, signatures, page photographs or mockup shadows in the illustration.
Export the physical cover separately using the printer's template for the final page count and paper.
For the current 96-page story book, retain the established arrangement of story illustrations on odd pages 9–87 with blank reverses, and four activity pages on odd pages 89–95 with blank reverses. Do not place captions on the blank backs.
Blank reverses help preserve the next illustration; they do not make the paper marker-proof. Paper and binding claims must match the product actually manufactured.
Prompt architecture
Compile the scene instead of copying the manual
The prompt should tell the image tool what to draw, how the characters interact and which drawing qualities matter most. Use the same four labels as the original brief. A useful starting length is about 150–250 words for an ordinary page, with more only when necessary. This is a house editing target, not a model limit.
Put the visible event first. Follow it with one particular observation, the selected composition and the essential ink treatment. Include a short restriction list that addresses plausible failures for this page. The full document's hard requirements remain the directing agent's review checklist.
Compact four-part template
Text
Core Subject:
[Approved cast] [specific action] in [place].
[One detail that follows from this action].
Image 1 controls character identity; Image 2 controls accepted ink style.

Style Injection:
Original children's colouring-page ink drawing. Confident rounded felt-tip
contours, broad soft forms, slight purposeful asymmetry, large white fill
areas, black ink on white. Preserve the approved characters and ink profile.

Geometry & Composition:
[Viewpoint and large setting shape]. [Body direction, gaze and prop contact].
[Two necessary setting anchors]. [Quiet area].
Keep visible colourable boundaries closed and suppress hidden rear outlines.
Leave lettering for a separate layout step.

Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt):
No shading or gray fills, scratchy texture, duplicated contours, tiny filler,
changed mascot anatomy, pseudo-text or photographed-page background.
[Only the most relevant page-specific failure to prevent].
Reference numbers must match the inputs actually supplied. If there is only one input, identify that input and omit an invented second reference. If the tool does not support a separate negative-prompt field, include the restriction prose in the main prompt. Use only documented parameters.
A complete compact prompt
Text
Core Subject:
Miso kneads a broad mound of dough while Pip peers over one mixing bowl.
Two small flour patches sit on Pip's star body. The attached mascot sheet
controls their identity; the accepted pilot controls the line character.

Style Injection:
Original bold children's colouring page. Confident rounded felt-tip ink,
soft broad forms and a few deliberate shape differences. Black on white,
with clean enclosed fill areas and no surface rendering.

Geometry & Composition:
Use a shallow front view across one broad worktop. Miso leans toward the
dough, with rounded paws visibly pressing it; Pip looks toward the bowl.
Include a rolling pin and one cupboard block. Keep the wall open and both
faces clear. Match the accepted character scale and ink profile.

Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt):
No shading, gray fills, tiny patterns, extra star points, human fingers,
pseudo-labels, doubled ink or photographic paper.
The exact placement of the cast can change if the story does not constrain it. The garden room's recurring door and window placement remains a continuity requirement.
Generation and edit are different instructions
For a new illustration, references supply identity and drawing principles; create the requested original scene. For an edit, identify the existing target and state the change plus the features that must stay fixed. Repeat important invariants on every edit.
Do not use a full-scene regeneration to repair one cup handle if a targeted edit is supported. Do not claim a local edit stayed local without inspecting the returned image.
Avoid competing style cues
Do not combine “perfect geometric vector,” “rough sketch,” “painted texture,” “ultra-thick border,” “delicate pen” and “clean felt tip” in one request. Choose a coherent ink family. Use the approved pilot to settle disputes about visual style.
Do not include a litany of competitor names in the style prompt. Describe the observed drawing decisions and supply approved original references. Do not pile up adjectives such as premium, beautiful, handmade and professional in place of a scene.
When the user provides a specific subject, preserve it. When the brief is open, add only the small observation and setting details needed to make the action coherent. The scene bank supplies options, not permission to add unrelated props or characters to a tightly specified request.
Six generation examples
These are original subject briefs for this cast. Combine each with the four-part style and restrictions above. For a new edition of the existing story, keep the manuscript and continuity decisions from the book plan.
Example 1 — Flour on Pip
Core Subject: Miso kneads one large mound of dough while Pip peers over the mixing bowl with two small outlined flour patches on the star body. One tiny star-shaped piece of dough sits beside Miso's broad round bun.
Style Injection: Use the approved cast, heavy smooth marker ink, rounded geometry and large white fill areas. The humour comes from the scale and the flour, with calm expressions.
Geometry & Composition: Use a shallow front view across one broad worktop. Put Miso on the left, Pip on the right and the mixing bowl between them without hiding either face. Include only a rolling pin and one cupboard block behind. Keep the wall mostly open. Show both paws contacting the dough clearly.
Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt): No kitchen inventory wall, tiny cookie army, oven glow, flour stipple, pseudo-labels, extra star points, human fingers or shading.
Example 2 — One good berry
Core Subject: Sprout carries one broad berry on a flat leaf tray resting securely across the shell. The berry looks large beside the snail, and Sprout looks quietly pleased.
Style Injection: Use the approved snail silhouette with a clear spiral, two eye stalks and no hands. Keep the finish bold, minimal and hand-swept.
Geometry & Composition: Use a low viewpoint and make Sprout large in the foreground. Include one rounded chair leg and a floor line to establish scale. Leave the upper background clear. Keep the tray supported and separate from the spiral line. Use only a few broad berry marks.
Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt): No face on the berry, tiny seeds, detached shell, extra antennae, arms, dark floor shadow, garden clutter or photographic texture.
Example 3 — The room behind the door
Core Subject: Miso and Sprout look through the low doorway into the garden room. The room has a crescent-shaped window, a bench and a low table, with room to imagine their picnic.
Style Injection: Use a clean black-and-white children's colouring page with confident thick outlines and gentle asymmetry. Keep the room inviting through arrangement.
Geometry & Composition: Frame the view from just outside the left-hand door. Put Miso to one side of the threshold and Sprout at floor level. Keep the crescent window on the rear wall. Use a broad empty floor between the cast and the table. Maintain the approved room layout in later scenes.
Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt): No gothic doorway, hidden clutter, dramatic perspective, gray interior, magical light beams, tiny floorboards or furniture fused to the wall.
Example 4 — An umbrella over the wagon
Core Subject: Plum holds one broad umbrella above Sprout and the covered picnic basket in the small wagon. Sprout's berry tray is tucked safely beside the basket.
Style Injection: Use soft rounded shapes, heavy felt-tip contours and a clean white sky. Suggest the rainy moment with a few spaced drops.
Geometry & Composition: Use a side view with Plum beside the wagon and a clear path leading toward the porch. Show the shaft connected to Plum's paw and the canopy. Keep the wagon wheels aligned and the handle distinct. Let the umbrella be the largest shape. Do not put a shaft or raindrop across a face.
Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt): No storm danger, dense rain hatching, transparent gray umbrella, extra wheels, floating basket, disconnected paws or crowded garden.
Example 5 — A place beside the book
Core Subject: Plum reads a large picture book while Miso settles onto a floor cushion. Plum has left a clear little space beside the book for Sprout's tiny cup, though Sprout is not visible in this scene.
Style Injection: Use the approved characters and a calm original reading scene, with broad unfilled shapes and nearly uniform rounded marker outlines.
Geometry & Composition: Use a quiet two-character composition beneath one round window. Make the book and the cushion broad. Show a simple crescent and tree inside the book without letters. Include one folded blanket. Keep a clear gap between Miso's ear and the window frame.
Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt): No extra visible character, library wall full of tiny books, text, micro-knit, fur texture, patterned wallpaper, shaded window or perfect mirrored poses.
Example 6 — Passing the first bun
Core Subject: Miso passes one bun to Plum at the indoor picnic. The spotted berry bowl sits nearby, and three slightly different paper moons hang below the crescent window.
Style Injection: Create an original colouring master with heavy black contours and large clean regions. Use the simple hand-inked finish of the accepted pilot.
Geometry & Composition: Use the established garden-room layout, one low table and a broad blanket. Keep a clear giver-item-receiver path. The bun touches the giving paw naturally; avoid piling both characters' paws into one black knot. Rain appears only beyond the window. Keep the food count modest.
Strict Restrictions (Negative Prompt): No glowing candle, gradients, extra hands, moving window, dense blanket print, tiny snack buffet, gray rain wash or copied competitor staging.
