Book continuity
Keep a small continuity record
For each accepted page, record scene ID, story beat, cast, temporary accessories, prop versions, room version, weather, direction of travel, composition and difficulty. Note any item that must persist into the next scene.
For The Little Moon Picnic, keep these anchors stable:
A simple basket, spotted berry bowl, plain blanket with a broad border and a small wagon.
Sprout's leaf tray and tiny cup.
Three paper moons made during preparation and used later in the room.
Garden-room door on the left, crescent window on the rear wall, low table and bench.
A clear progression from preparation to rain, shelter, decorating, supper and moonlight.
The first drawing depicts an outdoor picnic; the later drawing depicts the indoor picnic.
Do not change the window from crescent to round, silently add a fireplace or move a door merely because a later composition is difficult. Change the viewpoint or simplify the shot.
Variety without breaking the story
Alternate wider scenes with close actions and quiet portraits. Avoid three consecutive pages with the same cast arrangement, viewpoint and main prop. Adjacent scenes in the same room can differ through framing and action rather than a new room design.
Use group scenes at meaningful moments. Avoid showing all four characters and the whole inventory on every page. A warm close view of one friend is useful pacing.
Search motif
The proposed optional motif is one tiny smiling leaf per story illustration. Add it as a consistent layout detail after the main art is accepted. Keep one recognizable leaf with a simple face; ordinary plant leaves do not count.
Place it where it does not create a false body part or interrupt a fill boundary. Do not put it on a character's face. Use one per scene and keep its size large enough to recognize on paper. It is a secondary search detail, not a required intricate colouring area.
Production workflow
Stage 1 — Establish one reliable reference set
Reuse the approved mascot sheet, story and accepted art. Establish missing turn views, recurring room geometry and a small prop set only when needed. Existing approval persists; do not make the user approve the same design again.
For a new book, select one representative pilot that includes a recognizable face, a physical interaction and an environment boundary. A visually attractive page with no difficult contacts is not enough to establish whether the workflow can draw the rest of the book.
Stage 2 — Plan the large shapes
Write the event in one sentence. Establish body direction, contact and support. Choose the main silhouette, a framing shape and a quiet area. Decide which background lines stop behind the characters. Check room and prop continuity.
Use a brief internal shape plan. Generate a separate thumbnail image only if a difficult scene genuinely needs composition exploration or the user requested options. Routine pages do not need a gallery of extra variants.
Stage 3 — Calibrate the ink once
Apply the line-work calibration to the pilot at actual placed size. Choose the boldest comfortable treatment that preserves the reference character and broad white regions. If numeric stroke control or a physical proof is unavailable, record that limitation rather than guessing a measurement.
Keep the accepted pilot and ink profile stable through the batch. Do not make global brush changes in the middle of a book without revisiting already accepted pages.
Stage 4 — Generate or draw the page
Use the actual image tool and compact prompt. Attach the relevant references through the tool's supported mechanism. For a manual drawing workflow, use long intentional ink contours and a modest smoothing setting that preserves shape. Exact brush controls depend on the artist and application. [S10]
For a new scene, aim first for sound staging and identity. Perfectly clean ink around a wrong pose is still a wrong drawing. If an internal exploratory sketch is used, the final master must remove all construction marks.
Stage 5 — Inspect in order
Story and silhouette: Is the requested event visible? Does the main pose work without decoration?
Identity and interaction: Are the mascots correct? Do the paws, prop, gaze and support agree?
Ink and colourability: Are contours confident, boundaries closed and white areas comfortable?
Continuity and presentation: Are the cast count, room, prop versions, crop and output mode correct?
Fix the largest failure first. Do not polish a border while the character has an extra limb or the action is unreadable.
Stage 6 — Make focused repairs
Name one defect category, specify the visible correction and repeat the important invariants. Inspect after every edit. Keep the last better version. If an edit fixes one handle but damages the face, return to the prior version and narrow the instruction.
If the same defect persists through two focused attempts, stop repeating the same request. Simplify the contact, change the crop, correct the reference or rebuild the pose. This is a strategy-change trigger, not an arbitrary requirement to abandon requested work after a fixed number of calls.
Use direct geometry correction where an authorized drawing workflow supports it. Thickening, sharpening or upscaling a malformed shape is not an anatomy repair.
Stage 7 — Typeset and verify the actual deliverable
Add the exact caption and optional search motif separately. Check final placement, spelling, margins and effective resolution. Preserve blank backs. Keep the cover and coloured preview separate from the line-art interior.
Perform the applicable digital fill tests. Print representative pages where proofing is available and test with the intended materials and readers. Otherwise deliver the best reviewed digital stage with the missing proof identified. Do not invent a paper test, claim production verification from a screen view or create an unnecessary permission checkpoint.
Six pilot subjects for the book
The six examples exercise different problems: a shared work surface, Sprout's anatomy and scale, a room reveal, umbrella and wagon connections, a quiet reading pose and a food handoff. Use those differences to inspect capability. Do not claim the workflow performs well because one attractive portrait succeeded.
Quality review
Review what is visible
Describe the page before reading its caption: “Miso is pressing dough while Pip watches the bowl.” If the reviewer can name only a character and a room, the action needs improvement. This is an editorial check, not a blind experiment unless the caption was actually hidden.
Use the same viewing scale and reference pilot when comparing pages. Judge the drawing rather than the sophistication of its prompt.
Mandatory art checks

Correct mode: no intentional colour, gray fill or shading in the colouring master.

The requested event, cast and important object counts are correct.

Mascot silhouettes and faces match the identity reference.

Pip retains five-point construction; Sprout has an attached shell, two eye stalks and no hands.

Body direction, gaze, physical contact and support make the action coherent.

The pose is expressive without breaking the approved anatomy.

Faces and main contact points are free of accidental tangencies or black junction knots.

Intended fill regions are visibly closed; hidden rear outlines do not cross foreground shapes.

The actual-size composition has usable white regions and no unnecessary tiny pockets.

The contours read as confident hand-swept ink, with no fake jitter, ghost strokes or mechanical repeated stamps.

The particular story detail is visible; unrelated decorative filler has been removed.

Room geometry, props, weather and accessories match continuity.

No pseudo-writing, borrowed logo, watermark or photographed-page background.

Framing and lettering placement fit the actual requested output.
An untested requirement must remain not_tested; writing it on this checklist does not count as a pass. A failed identity, anatomy, action or fill-boundary requirement cannot be compensated for with a high aesthetic score.
Three viewing distances
Thumbnail: use about 160–200 pixels of page width as an initial review scale. Can the action and focal group be identified? Are the main silhouettes clear? The small face need not be individually readable at this scale.
Actual size: inspect the intended printed dimensions, either in an actual-size display workflow or on paper. Check fill areas, captions, narrow gaps and ink weight. A print-like screen preview is not a paper proof; record which was used.
Close view: inspect eye dots, paw contacts, cup handles, star points, shell attachments, occlusion joins and accidental duplicated edges. Normal antialiasing at a crisp contour is different from an intentionally gray region.
Evidence of a handmade-looking result
Before accepting a page, identify at least three visible reasons it feels drawn with intent. They can include a purposeful gesture, a confident long curve, slight surface compression, a meaningful asymmetry or a small detail that follows from the action. These are observations, not instructions to add three decorative imperfections.
Examples: the head turns toward the book; the cushion compresses under Miso; the scarf hangs with the lean. If all the reviewer can report is “cute face, thick lines, many props,” revise the staging.
House review rubric
Criterion	Points	Evidence to record
Character consistency	20	Correct visible silhouette, face and accessories against the reference
Action and emotional clarity	20	An identifiable event supported by pose, gaze and contact
Composition and shape hierarchy	15	Clear first read, distinct silhouettes and an intentional quiet area
Line quality and handmade appearance	15	Confident curves, clean joins and purposeful shape differences
Colourability	20	Broad usable regions, clean visible closure and any completed fill test
Originality and finish	10	A particular original moment, coherent props and resolved visible defects
Total	100	
Use 85/100 as a subjective internal target, together with the mandatory checks. Record the defect behind each deduction. Scores compare versions within this project; they do not measure children's preferences or certify that an image was handmade.
Verification states
Status	Required evidence
DRAFT	A useful candidate exists; one or more relevant art or output checks remain unresolved or untested
ART_REVIEWED	Actual image inspected at thumbnail, actual placed size and close view; mandatory visual checks pass; required digital fill check passes if the deliverable promises digital fill compatibility
PRINT_VERIFIED	The final assembled file has passed dimension, effective-resolution and margin checks, and an actual representative physical proof has been inspected; record the proof scope
A reviewed digital image can be delivered as ART_REVIEWED while physical proofing is pending. Do not call it PRINT_VERIFIED. Internal review is not a claim that the user has approved the page. Any later change to a verified file requires rechecking the affected properties; a material global change requires a new proof before print verification is restored.
Common failure families
Visible failure	Diagnostic question	Next change
Rigid clip-art pose	Is there a readable direction of weight, attention and contact?	Turn or lean the body toward the action; preserve the approved face
Inflated or melted forms	Has rounding erased a functional edge or mascot feature?	Restore a purposeful silhouette and simplify the contact
Overly mechanical drawing	Are paired and repeated shapes identical throughout?	Adjust a few meaningful shape or placement relationships
Scratchy fake handwork	Is irregularity high-frequency noise rather than a chosen contour?	Replace noisy segments with longer confident sweeps
Black heavy knots	Are too many contours crossing one contact?	Simplify the interaction and separate the readable boundaries
Richness became clutter	Do small props distract from the event?	Keep the setting anchors and story detail; remove redundant inventory
Minimalism became emptiness	Is a tiny subject stranded on a large page?	Enlarge the action and establish a broad framing shape
Inconsistent batch	Did the reference, ink profile or face construction change?	Return to the original pilot and identity sheet
Local edit damaged good work	Were invariants omitted or the edit scope too broad?	Restore the previous version and narrow the change
Apparently sharp but unprintable	Was the file enlarged or only its DPI tag changed?	Inspect native pixels at final placement; obtain adequate source detail
Repair instructions
Use a focused edit instruction that names the defect and the geometry to preserve. These templates do not guarantee a local edit; inspect the returned result.
Repair a leaking boundary
“Keep the composition, cast, expression and line weight. Close the gap where [rear shape] meets [foreground shape]. The foreground outline must complete the boundary. Remove the hidden rear line through the foreground object. Do not add shading or redraw unaffected areas.”
Repair sterile geometry
“Keep the approved identities and closed regions. Give [head/cushion/cup] a slightly hand-swept contour and modest asymmetry. Vary the placement of [two props] deliberately. Keep the strokes smooth and confident. Do not add roughness, jitter, paper grain or sketch marks.”
Repair crowded composition
“Keep [main action], [cast] and [setting anchor]. Remove [named redundant props] and the small background patterns. Enlarge [important prop]. Restore a broad quiet area at [location]. Maintain the existing primary stroke weight and do not add replacement decorations.”
Repair a mascot
“Match [mascot] to the attached approved sheet, especially [specific silhouette, face, ears, scarf, points or shell]. Keep the current action and correct prop contact. Change only the incorrect identity features. Do not replace the whole cast with a generic cute animal style.”
Repair a physical interaction
“Redraw the contact between [character] and [prop]. Show [one clear connection] and a stable support surface. Separate the overlapping outlines so the reader can distinguish the paw, handle and object. Preserve the face and the rest of the scene.”
Repair tiny colouring pockets
“Keep the major silhouettes and story. Merge the small enclosed regions on [object] into a few broad areas. Remove unnecessary seams, parallel rims and miniature patterns. Keep the functional feature that identifies the object. Do not solve the problem by shrinking the whole drawing.”
Restore the colouring master
“Keep the accepted drawing's contours and object arrangement. Remove all intentional gray or coloured fills, shadows, highlights, surface texture and lighting effects. Leave clean black ink on white with smooth edges. Preserve faces as small solid black marks.”
Repair a rigid pose
“Keep [mascot]'s approved face, silhouette features and accessory design. Turn the head and body toward [action]. Put the weight on [surface] and make [paw or star point] visibly contact [prop]. Let the scarf or cushion respond to that movement. Keep the same cast and environment. Do not add extra limbs, decoration or texture.”
Repair an overly heavy contact
“Keep the accepted primary line character. Simplify the join between [paw], [handle] and [object] so each form remains readable at final size. Enlarge the inner handle opening or remove a redundant contour. Preserve closed regions and the approved face; do not thin the entire picture or add a second border.”
Recover from edit drift
“Use the previous accepted image as the edit target. Change only [specific visible defect]. Preserve the exact cast, their facial construction, accepted pose, key object counts, major room layout and ink treatment. Do not repeat the broader restyling that changed [identified good feature].”
After any repair, inspect the returned image and repeat the affected mandatory checks. Compare against the last better version. A local change can accidentally alter a face, introduce an extra limb or reopen a nearby boundary.
